## Appendix A Light curve calculation

At time t=t_{f} - the end of the simulations, most of the outflow reaches homologous expansion. This enables us to advance the outflow expansion in post-processing using the adiabatic relations. We note that because we do not consider emission from the jet, we can safely ignore the magnetic component which is negligible outside of the jet. The emission calculation is performed on the unbound 3D outflow as follows. At each line of sight and time, we find the photosphere R_{\\rm ph}(t,\\theta,\\phi)=R\\left[\\tau(t,R_{\\rm ph},\\theta,\\phi)=1\\right], and the trapping radius R_{\\rm fr}(t,\\theta,\\phi)=R\\left[\\tau(t,R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)=c/v(t_{f},R_{ \\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)\\right], where v is the velocity, and \\tau is the optical depth along a radial line of sight:

\\tau(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)=\\left(\\frac{t}{t_{f}}\\right)^{-2}\\int_{r}^{\\infty}\\kappa (\\theta)\\rho(t_{f},r^{\\prime},\\theta,\\phi)(1-v(t_{f},r^{\\prime},\\theta,\\phi)/c )\\gamma(t_{f},r^{\\prime},\\theta,\\phi)\\mathrm{d}r^{\\prime}\\,, (A1) where \\rho is the mass density.

The photons diffuse out from the trapping radius, such that the luminosity per solid angle in the lab frame due to cooling emission is:

L_{\\rm c}(t,\\theta,\\phi)=\\left[4p(t_{f},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)+AQ(t,r,\\theta, \\phi)\\rho(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)\\right]\\gamma(t_{f},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)^{2}\\left( \\frac{t_{f}}{t}\\right)^{4}v(t_{f},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)\\,R_{\\rm tr}(t,\\theta,\\phi)^{2}\\,, (A2) where p is the thermal pressure, \\gamma is the Lorentz factor, and the heating rate contribution to cooling due to \\beta and \\gamma radioactive decay is (Hotokezaka et al., 2016):

A=1-\\exp\\left[-\\left(\\frac{\\gamma(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)}{t_{d}}\\right)^{2}\\right]+0. 5;\\;\\;\\dot{Q}(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)=10^{10}\\left[\\frac{t_{d}}{\\gamma(t,r,\\theta,\\phi )}\\right]^{-1.3}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{erg}}{\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{s}}\\,, (A3) and may vary with the composition to within a factor of a few (see e.g., Freiburghaus et al., 1999; Metzger et al., 2010; Korobkin et al., 2012; Wanajo et al., 2014; Hotokezaka et al., 2016). The time t_{d} is defined as t_{d}\\equiv t/\\mathrm{day}. The first term in (A2) is the adiabatic cooling of the shocked cocoon, as obtained from the radial flux component of the stress-energy tensor. The second term accounts for the radioactive heating of the gas. Both contributions are calculated in the comoving frame, and then boosted to the lab frame. The luminosity per solid angle due to radioactive decay in the lab frame is:

L_{r}(t,\\theta,\\phi)=\\int_{R_{\\rm tr}}^{\\infty}A\\dot{Q}(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)\\rho(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)\\gamma(t,r,\\theta,\\phi)^{2}r^{2}\\mathrm{d}r\\,, (A4)

The total local luminosity is the sum of both contributions, L(t,\\theta,\\phi)=L_{\\rm c}(t,\\theta,\\phi)+L_{r}(t,\\theta,\\phi). The local temperature is calculated at the photosphere and assumed to be blackbody.

To transform the emission from the lab frame luminosity to the observed frame (\\theta_{\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs}), we find:

t_{\\rm obs}(\\theta,\\phi)=t\\left[1-v(t_{f},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)/c\\mu(\\theta_ {\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs}\\theta,\\phi)\\right]\\,, (A5) where:

\\mu(\\theta_{\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs},\\theta,\\phi)=\\cos\\theta{\\rm cos}\\theta_{ \\rm obs}+\\sin\\theta_{\\rm obs}\\sin\\theta{\\rm cos}(\\phi-\\phi_{\\rm obs})\\,. (A6)

Then, the observed luminosity is:

L_{\\rm obs}(t_{\\rm obs},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta_{\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs})=\\int_{0}^{ \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}D(t_{\\rm obs},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)^{4}\\gamma(t_{\\rm obs},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta,\\phi)L(t_{\\rm obs},\\theta,\\phi)\\mathrm{d}\\mu(\\theta_{\\rm obs },\\phi_{\\rm obs},\\theta,\\phi)\\,, (A7) where D(t_{\\rm obs},R_{\\rm tr},\\theta_{\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs})=\\left[\\gamma(t_{\\rm obs },R_{\\rm tr},\\theta_{\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs})\\left(1-v(t_{\\rm obs},R_{\\rm tr}, \\theta_{\\rm obs},\\phi_{\\rm obs})\\right)\\right]^{-1} is the Doppler factor of the emitting gas.

The emission peak time is derived as follows. The photons diffuse out from the trapping radius, \\tau(R_{\\rm tr})=c/v, thus Eq. (A1) dictates:

\\frac{3M\\kappa}{4\\pi(vt)^{2}}\\gamma(1-v/c)\\approx\\frac{c}{v}\\,. (A8)

Approximating Eq. (A5) to t_{\\rm obs}\\approx t(1-v/c), we find:

t_{\\rm obs}\\approx\\left[\\frac{\\kappa M\\gamma}{4vc}\\left(1-\\frac{v}{c}\\right)^{ 3}\\right]^{0.5}\\,. (A9)